# GCP project Starfish runs in: its Firestore store. Gemini also runs here unless
# STARFISH_VERTEX_PROJECT_ID says otherwise (enable the Vertex AI API in whichever it is).
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=

# GCP project whose Cloud Logging is read for errors. Unset: GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT
# (Starfish watching the project it runs in). One watched project per deployment:
# change STARFISH_ENV with it so two projects' incidents never share a label.
# STARFISH_WATCHED_PROJECT_ID=

# GCP project Gemini runs in (and is billed to). Unset: GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT. Set it to
# run Gemini in a different project from Starfish's store.
# STARFISH_VERTEX_PROJECT_ID=

# Vertex AI region.
GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION=us-central1

# Gemini model used for triage, fix drafting and adversarial review.
STARFISH_MODEL=gemini-3.1-pro-preview

# Label stamped on every incident. Environments must never share a value —
# this is what stops a dev incident from being treated as a production one.
STARFISH_ENV=dev

# Firestore collection holding incidents, and the database holding it. Starfish uses
# its own named database ("starfish") so its rules and indexes never touch your app's;
# create it once: gcloud firestore databases create --database=starfish --location=<region>
# "(default)" opts back into the default database.
STARFISH_INCIDENT_COLLECTION=incidents
# Collection holding the runtime settings document an admin edits from the UI. The
# values in this file are the defaults; a valid settings document overrides the
# watched project, environment label, log resource types, service patterns and
# noise list. Unset: starfish_settings.
# STARFISH_SETTINGS_COLLECTION=starfish_settings

# Engine-only collection mapping each (environment, fingerprint) to its incident, so
# concurrent ingest runs converge on one document. Unset: incident_keys.
# STARFISH_INCIDENT_KEY_COLLECTION=incident_keys
# STARFISH_DATABASE_ID=starfish

# Substring -> service attribution, "pattern:service,pattern:service".
# A function matching nothing resolves to "platform" rather than being
# attributed to whichever service happens to be listed first.
STARFISH_SERVICE_PATTERNS=

# How far back the first aggregation run looks when no checkpoint exists.
STARFISH_LOOKBACK_HOURS=24

# The command that proves a proposed patch is acceptable. It runs in a scratch copy of the target
# repo (without .git) with the patch applied; exit 0 is the only pass. Unset: no patch is ever
# accepted. Runs with a scrubbed environment, but it is NOT a sandbox: run it on a disposable runner.
# STARFISH_VERIFY_COMMAND="pnpm install --frozen-lockfile && pnpm run verify"
# STARFISH_VERIFY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=600
# Paths a patch may never touch, added to the built-in list (.git/, .github/, .claude/, .env*, keys and
# credential files, node_modules/, package.json and the npm/pnpm/yarn lockfiles, and vitest/vite/jest/karma/
# playwright/mocha, tsconfig and eslint configs). Comma-separated; a trailing "/" is a directory, "*" and "**" are
# globs, a pattern with no "/" matches that name at any depth. List any OTHER file that defines the verify command
# or the tests that judge it (a Makefile, pytest.ini, a test helper), or a patch can edit the judge.
# STARFISH_PATCH_EXCLUDED_PATHS=

# Ingest windows end this many minutes behind real time, because Cloud Logging makes an
# entry searchable a little after it happened. Unset: 5.
# STARFISH_INGEST_LAG_MINUTES=5
# The longest span one window covers; catching up after downtime is done in windows of this
# size. Unset: 6.
# STARFISH_INGEST_MAX_WINDOW_HOURS=6
# The Cloud Scheduler cron that starts the ingest, so the UI can say when the next run is expected
# (scripts/deploy-gcp.sh and scripts/ingest-cadence.sh set it). Unset: the UI says nothing about the next run.
# STARFISH_INGEST_SCHEDULE="0 * * * *"
# Functions and services whose errors are never ingested, comma-separated, matched ignoring case and
# with `_` read as `-`. Put Starfish's own Cloud Run service or job name here: when it shares a
# project with the app it watches, it would otherwise read its own errors and the fixer could be
# asked to patch Starfish itself. A trailing * excludes a prefix (`control-*`, `stub-*`). Unset: starfish. Set to empty to exclude nothing (not advised).
# STARFISH_EXCLUDED_SERVICES=starfish

# Operator records, which the engine reads to check that an admin action really comes from an
# admin, and the audit trail of those actions. Unset: starfish_users, starfish_audit. The rules
# name both, like the other collections.
# STARFISH_USERS_COLLECTION=starfish_users
# STARFISH_AUDIT_COLLECTION=starfish_audit

# Dry run: the agent loop drafts and verifies everything and opens nothing. ON by default, so
# nothing leaves Starfish until you set this to false on purpose. REVIEW_REQUIRED: a patch must
# survive the adversarial review before it can become a pull request. MODEL_CALL_BUDGET: the most
# model calls one cycle may make (triage, each fix attempt, each review).
# STARFISH_DRY_RUN=true
# STARFISH_REVIEW_REQUIRED=true
# The kill switch: false stops the agent loop at its next step. Unset: true. An admin can also flip it on the
# Settings page, and it is read fresh before every stage (src/agent/automationSwitch.ts).
# STARFISH_AUTOMATION_ENABLED=true
# The agent entrypoint (`pnpm agent`, src/agentCli.ts, #74) refuses to start unless this is set. none (the
# default): the verify command runs nowhere, so the agent cannot run. in-process: it runs the target repo's
# own tests on THIS machine as THIS identity: not a sandbox. Use it only on a machine with no credentials the
# tested code must not reach (a laptop with none set, not the engine's Cloud Run job). MAX_CYCLES_PER_PASS:
# the most cycles one pass starts. Unset: none, 3.
# cloud-run-job: the verify command runs in a SEPARATE Cloud Run job whose service account holds no roles; the
# agent packs the source and patch into STARFISH_VERIFY_BUCKET, starts STARFISH_VERIFY_JOB (in
# STARFISH_VERIFY_JOB_REGION, unset: GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION) with per-run signed URLs, and waits for the result
# (docs/deploy.md). scripts/deploy-gcp.sh sets all three with DEPLOY_AGENT=1.
# github-actions: the patch is pushed to a starfish/<cycle> branch in STARFISH_GITHUB_REPO and the TARGET's own
# CI, on GitHub's disposable runners, is the judge: STARFISH_VERIFY_CHECKS names the check runs that must all pass.
# The agent never runs the target's code. The target's workflow must run on `push` to `starfish/**` and should hold
# no secrets (a patch's code runs in it); a branch is pushed even in a dry run, and in a dry run it is deleted once
# CI has judged it, passed or not (the check runs stay on the commit; docs/deploy.md).
# Only runs posted by STARFISH_VERIFY_CHECK_APP (a GitHub App's slug; unset: github-actions) count as those checks,
# here and wherever the named checks are judged (reconcile, auto-merge, fast-revert): a run of the same name from any
# other GitHub App is ignored. The slug tells apps apart, not workflows: every workflow in the target repository posts
# as github-actions, so any of them can post a run of that name. Every run of a named check on the commit (all of
# them, re-runs included) must succeed; a commit with more runs than Starfish reads is never judged as passed.
# scripts/deploy-gcp.sh sets it on the agent job from VERIFY_CHECK_APP (#229).
# STARFISH_VERIFY_ISOLATION=none
# STARFISH_VERIFY_CHECKS=
# STARFISH_VERIFY_CHECK_APP=github-actions
# STARFISH_VERIFY_JOB=
# STARFISH_VERIFY_BUCKET=
# STARFISH_VERIFY_JOB_REGION=
# STARFISH_MAX_CYCLES_PER_PASS=3
# The most cycles started per UTC day in this environment (a new cycle, or a declined one reconsidered); at the cap a
# pass starts nothing until the next day. Unset: 30.
# STARFISH_MAX_CYCLES_PER_DAY=30
# A declined incident is reconsidered only on new evidence: an operator's retriage request (from the UI), its
# occurrences growing by BURST since the decline, or a new structural cause (error code/name/HTTP status, or a detail or
# stack appearing). The automatic triggers wait COOLDOWN minutes after the decline; at most MAX_PER_DAY
# reconsiderations per cycle in 24 hours. Unset: 30, 3, 20.
# STARFISH_RECONSIDER_COOLDOWN_MINUTES=30
# STARFISH_RECONSIDER_MAX_PER_DAY=3
# STARFISH_RECONSIDER_BURST_OCCURRENCES=20
# STARFISH_MODEL_CALL_BUDGET=6

# Replay harness (`pnpm replay`, #10, scoreboard/FORMAT.md): runs the agent's stages over a corpus and writes one
# results file for `pnpm scoreboard`. Model runs need STARFISH_VERIFY_ISOLATION=in-process (the corpus's commands and
# the model's patches run in this process: not a sandbox). A case running longer than the timeout is recorded as one.
# STARFISH_REPLAY_CORPUS=scoreboard/corpus
# STARFISH_REPLAY_RESULTS_DIR=scoreboard/results
# STARFISH_REPLAY_CASE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=1800
# The agent loop works one cycle per incident and recurrence. A worker holds a lease on its
# cycle for this long before another may take it over (a crashed worker's cycle is resumed); a
# working worker renews it about every third of that, through a verify wait too. The cycle as a
# whole has a deadline the lease can never extend, and is given up on after this many starts
# (a person is told when Slack is set). A released cycle is retried by the NEXT agent pass, so keep
# the deadline at several agent schedule intervals; the agent refuses to start with one shorter
# than twice (verify wait + lease) or a lease under 60 s. Collection holds the cycles and their run
# events. Unset: 300 s, 360 min, 3.
# STARFISH_CYCLE_COLLECTION=starfish_cycles
# STARFISH_LEASE_SECONDS=300
# STARFISH_CYCLE_DEADLINE_MINUTES=360
# STARFISH_MAX_CYCLE_ATTEMPTS=3
# Collection for the ingest checkpoint and the last-run record the UI shows. Unset: starfish_runs.
# STARFISH_RUN_COLLECTION=starfish_runs

# The pull-request step (src/integrations/githubPr.ts). It opens pull requests in ONE repository,
# owner/name, from a branch named starfish/<cycle id> that it pushes (never force, never the base
# branch); it never merges. Empty repo: nothing can be opened. The token is a secret and is read
# only from STARFISH_GITHUB_TOKEN (Secret Manager on Cloud Run, docs/deploy.md), never from a
# setting. Limiting the token to starfish/* branches is a branch-protection / ruleset job on the
# repository: the engine checks it too, but a GitHub App cannot be scoped to a branch prefix.
# The same repo and token also file ONE issue per incident the agent acts on or escalates (label "starfish",
# links via STARFISH_UI_URL), so the token needs Issues: read and write too. A dry run files nothing.
# Unset: no repo, base branch main, https://api.github.com, https://github.com/<repo>.git,
# commits by "Starfish <starfish@users.noreply.github.com>".
# STARFISH_GITHUB_REPO=owner/name
# STARFISH_GITHUB_BASE_BRANCH=main
# STARFISH_GITHUB_API_URL=https://api.github.com
# STARFISH_GITHUB_REMOTE_URL=
# STARFISH_GITHUB_COMMIT_NAME=Starfish
# STARFISH_GITHUB_COMMIT_EMAIL=starfish@users.noreply.github.com
# STARFISH_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Slack (src/integrations/slack.ts): when a cycle ends waiting for a person (triage said notify; the fixer
# declined; the verify gate or the review would not pass a second time), the agent posts ONE message to ONE
# channel through an incoming webhook, with links back to the incident and its run. The webhook URL is a
# secret (anyone holding it can post to the channel): read only from STARFISH_SLACK_WEBHOOK_ONCALL (Secret
# Manager on Cloud Run, docs/deploy.md), never a setting, and refused unless it is a hooks.slack.com URL.
# STARFISH_UI_URL is the operator UI's origin (https://<site>.web.app) for those links; unset: no links.
# In a dry run (STARFISH_DRY_RUN, the default) nothing is sent and the cycle's record says so.
# STARFISH_SLACK_WEBHOOK_ONCALL=
# STARFISH_UI_URL=
# Optional second channel (the design's #ops-alerts): ONE "new incident" message for every cycle Starfish acts on or
# escalates (triage said act or notify; a declined incident gets none), with links back. Its own webhook, its own
# secret, the same rules as the on-call one; recorded on the cycle as summary.alert (pending/sent/failed).
# STARFISH_SLACK_WEBHOOK_ALERTS=

# Phone escalation (#37, src/oncall/): when an urgent cycle's "needs a human" Slack message goes unacknowledged for the
# on-call policy's wait (default 10 min), Starfish phones the on-call primary (again 2 min later), then the secondary,
# then the support lead, through Twilio. The call plays ONE recorded file with no incident detail; 1 takes it, 2 texts
# the link, 3 passes it on. OFF by default because a Twilio number and every call cost money: it also needs an admin
# to turn it on in Settings -> On-call, confirmed numbers there (each person reads back a texted code), and the dry run
# off (a dry run places nothing and records what it would do). Built, tested against a fake Twilio and the Firestore
# emulator; never run against real Twilio.
# STARFISH_PHONE_ESCALATION=false
# The recording every call plays (https, e.g. a public Cloud Storage object). Unset: no call can be placed.
# STARFISH_ONCALL_AUDIO_URL=
# The admin API's public origin, where Twilio posts its webhooks (signature-checked against this URL, never the Host
# header). Unset: no call can be placed.
# STARFISH_TWILIO_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Twilio's credentials: secrets, all three or none (Secret Manager on Cloud Run, docs/deploy.md), never a setting.
# STARFISH_TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
# STARFISH_TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# STARFISH_TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=
# Twilio's REST API root; only a test changes it.
# STARFISH_TWILIO_API_URL=https://api.twilio.com
# Where the policy, the people (admins only: personal data) and the call sequences live. Must match firestore.rules.
# STARFISH_ONCALL_COLLECTION=starfish_oncall
# STARFISH_ONCALL_PEOPLE_COLLECTION=starfish_oncall_people
# STARFISH_ESCALATION_COLLECTION=starfish_escalations

# Opened pull requests read back from GitHub each agent pass (src/agent/reconcile.ts): open, closed or merged, and the
# STARFISH_VERIFY_CHECKS runs on the PR's head, recorded on the cycle as summary.prState. Read-only; a merged PR is
# never taken as the incident healed. The most PRs one pass reads; unset: 10.
# STARFISH_MAX_PR_CHECKS_PER_PASS=10

# Cloud Logging resource types whose ERROR entries count, comma-separated.
# Unset: cloud_function,cloud_run_revision.
# STARFISH_LOG_RESOURCE_TYPES=

# ERROR-severity messages that are framework noise, "|"-separated. Unset keeps
# the callable-framework GET-probe defaults; set to empty to suppress nothing.
# STARFISH_IGNORED_MESSAGES=

# Cloud Monitoring failure signal (#19, src/ingest/monitoring.ts): failed invocations that wrote no ERROR log.
# Off by default; built and tested against fakes only, never run against real Cloud Monitoring. On, each ingest
# window also reads the watched project's failure metrics and needs roles/monitoring.viewer there
# (scripts/deploy-gcp.sh grants it when run with STARFISH_MONITORING_ENABLED=true; set it there, not in
# EXTRA_ENV_VARS). A read that fails is recorded as unavailable, never as zero failures, and is not retried.
# STARFISH_MONITORING_ENABLED=false
# Which built-in signals to read, comma-separated: cloud_run_revision (5xx request_count), cloud_function
# (non-ok execution_count). Unset: both. An unknown type is refused at startup.
# STARFISH_MONITORING_RESOURCE_TYPES=
# The most time series one window's read takes, across all signals; a capped read is reported. Unset: 500.
# STARFISH_MONITORING_MAX_SERIES=500

# Missing-index heal (#11, src/indexheal/createIndexUrl.ts): the project/database pairs a create-index URL
# may be acted on for, comma-separated, e.g. my-demo-project/(default). Empty (the default) allows nothing.
# Starfish's own database is refused even if listed.
# STARFISH_INDEX_ALLOWED_TARGETS=
# Creating the index is its own switch, off by default (and `autoMerge` never turns it on). It also needs
# STARFISH_DRY_RUN=false and the automation switch on. At most STARFISH_INDEX_DAILY_CAP are created per target per UTC day.
# STARFISH_INDEX_AUTO_APPLY=false
# STARFISH_INDEX_DAILY_CAP=3
# Before a create, the target database's composite indexes are counted (all collection groups, building ones too); at or
# above STARFISH_INDEX_MAX_PER_DATABASE nothing is created and a person decides. Keep it under Firestore's per-database limit.
# STARFISH_INDEX_MAX_PER_DATABASE=100
# The heal is only wired into `pnpm agent` when STARFISH_INDEX_ALLOWED_TARGETS lists a target; a MissingIndexError
# incident then takes the index path (no model) instead of triage. Before creating, the failing query must be known:
# STARFISH_INDEX_PROBES is a JSON array of queries an operator writes down, replayed read-only with a limit of one,
# e.g. [{"target":"my-demo/(default)","collection":"orders","where":[["status","==","open"]],"orderBy":[["createdAt","desc"]]}]
# ("scope":"COLLECTION_GROUP" for a collection-group query). Empty (the default): nothing can be confirmed, so nothing
# is created. After READY the same query must pass, and the incident must stay quiet for STARFISH_INDEX_OBSERVE_MINUTES
# before the run record says healed. STARFISH_INDEX_WAIT_MINUTES bounds one cycle's wait for READY (a later pass resumes).
# STARFISH_INDEX_FILE is the repository's index file the evidence pull request adds the entry to.
# STARFISH_INDEX_PROBES=
# STARFISH_INDEX_WAIT_MINUTES=10
# STARFISH_INDEX_OBSERVE_MINUTES=60
# STARFISH_INDEX_FILE=firestore.indexes.json

# Auto-merge (#9, src/agent/autoMerge.ts, docs/design/auto-merge-policy.md): built for the demo target, never run
# against real GitHub. Starfish never deploys. A pull request is merged only when ALL of these hold: the target
# (STARFISH_GITHUB_REPO) is listed in STARFISH_AUTO_MERGE_TARGETS (owner/name, comma-separated; environment-only, so
# list the demo repository and nothing else), STARFISH_AUTO_MERGE is on (an admin can also flip it in Settings),
# STARFISH_MERGE_FREEZE is off (the global off switch; also in Settings), STARFISH_DRY_RUN=false, the automation switch
# is on, both gates pass, and fewer than STARFISH_MAX_MERGES_PER_DAY merged today. A candidate whose required checks
# (STARFISH_VERIFY_CHECKS) are still running waits up to STARFISH_MERGE_CHECK_WAIT_MINUTES, then goes to a person.
# STARFISH_AUTO_MERGE_EXCLUDED_PATHS adds paths gate 1 never allows (same pattern syntax as the patch exclusions).
# Unset: off, off, none, none, 3, 120.
# STARFISH_AUTO_MERGE=false
# STARFISH_MERGE_FREEZE=false
# STARFISH_AUTO_MERGE_TARGETS=
# STARFISH_AUTO_MERGE_EXCLUDED_PATHS=
# STARFISH_MAX_MERGES_PER_DAY=3
# STARFISH_MERGE_CHECK_WAIT_MINUTES=120
# Fast-revert (src/agent/fastRevert.ts): the same incident seen again later than GRACE minutes after Starfish's merge
# and within MINUTES of it reverts that merge. The window starts at the MERGE (a deploy's completion is not observable
# yet): set the grace above the target's deploy time. Unset: 60, 10.
# STARFISH_FAST_REVERT_MINUTES=60
# STARFISH_FAST_REVERT_GRACE_MINUTES=10

# Admin API (src/adminApi.ts, #63, #65): the operator UI's origin(s), comma-separated, allowed to call it from a
# browser, e.g. https://my-site.web.app. Exact https origins only (http://localhost is accepted for local work).
# Empty (the default): no browser may; a server-side caller (the demo's reset script) is not affected by CORS.
# The server itself listens on PORT (Cloud Run sets it; default 8080).
# STARFISH_ADMIN_API_ORIGINS=
# Also require a valid Firebase App Check token (header X-Firebase-AppCheck) on its POST routes (#12). Default off.
# Turn on only when the UI is built with VITE_STARFISH_APPCHECK_SITE_KEY (web/.env.example); a call without a valid
# App Check token gets the same 401 as a bad ID token, so a UI without App Check could no longer use the admin API.
# STARFISH_ADMIN_API_APPCHECK=false
